Welcome to tmux!

tmux is a terminal multiplexer: it enables a number of terminals to be created,
accessed, and controlled from a single screen. tmux may be detached from a
screen and continue running in the background, then later reattached.

This is the Linux-DEX fork of next-3.9. Flagship differences from upstream:

	* side-status defaults to a one-column left strip: session names run
	  downward, then C/R/D/N for cpu, ram, disk and network rate
	* pane-border-type defaults to separate (full border around each pane);
	  Prefix+B cycles separate → separate-active → joined
	* status line branded "DEX" with path/host/mode cues; active borders
	  tint for scratch (yellow), floating (cyan), copy-mode (yellow)
	* Prefix+? opens a grouped key cheatsheet (which-key style)
	* Prefix+P opens a fuzzy command palette (switch-mode -C)
	* Prefix+h/j/k/l focus panes; Prefix+H/J/K/L swap with neighbors
	* Prefix+u toggles a floating scratch pane (non-modal; -s size preset)
	* Prefix+U toggles the floating pane layer (hide/show; PTYs keep running)
	* Prefix+= pins/unpins a floating pane (stays on top)
	* Prefix+M-s selects the stacked layout; M-n / M-p cycle panes
	* Prefix+F hint-jumps URLs/paths/hashes; Prefix+M-f opens the match
	* Prefix+S / BTab fuzzy session picker; Tab fuzzy window picker
	* Other-window bell + pane-exited: sound and desktop notification
	  (bell-action other, visual-bell both)
	* save-layout / load-layout persist shapes, cwds, titles and commands
	  (JSON v2; layout-restore-commands = prompt|auto|off; load-layout -y)
	* layout-autosave (default on, 60s) and save on client detach
	* upgrade-server saves layouts, stops the server, autoloads on restart
	  (pane processes exit; true PTY-preserving live-exec is not claimed)
	* example layouts under examples/layouts/
	* one-command install.sh (see below)

See example_tmux.conf for a short starter config.

* Install (one command)

macOS, Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora/RHEL, Arch, and openSUSE:

	$ curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Linux-DEX/tmux/main/install.sh | bash

Non-root install into ~/.local:

	$ curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Linux-DEX/tmux/main/install.sh | bash -s -- --no-sudo --yes

From a local clone:

	$ ./install.sh
	$ ./install.sh --prefix /usr/local --yes

The script installs build dependencies, builds next-3.9, and installs to
/usr/local (or ~/.local with --no-sudo). Run ./install.sh --help for options.

* Manual install (from source)

Requires libevent 2.x, ncurses, a C compiler, make, pkg-config, yacc or bison,
autoconf and automake:

	$ git clone https://github.com/Linux-DEX/tmux.git
	$ cd tmux
	$ sh autogen.sh
	$ ./configure && make
	$ sudo make install

On macOS, install deps with brew first. configure also requires an explicit
jemalloc choice (--enable-jemalloc is recommended):

	$ brew install libevent ncurses utf8proc pkg-config automake autoconf bison jemalloc
	$ ./configure --enable-jemalloc --enable-utf8proc && make
	$ sudo make install

Or build without jemalloc: ./configure --disable-jemalloc --disable-utf8proc && make

`make install` puts the binary in `/usr/local/bin/tmux` (next-3.9). It does
not replace a distro package at `/usr/bin/tmux`. If both exist, you can still
get the old version (borders/colors from this fork will be missing).

Remove the system package first, then install and restart any running server:

	# Arch:            sudo pacman -R tmux
	# Debian/Ubuntu:   sudo apt remove tmux
	# Fedora:          sudo dnf remove tmux
	# macOS (brew):    brew uninstall tmux

	$ hash -r
	$ tmux kill-server 2>/dev/null
	$ tmux -V          # expect: tmux next-3.9

`/usr/local/bin` must come before `/usr/bin` in `PATH` (usual default).
